        <body>
            <div xml:id="d841">

                <pb facs="0837" n="671" xml:id="pg671"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 711.5914/127</note>
                <head ana="#head_untitled-inferred-from-preceding-sibling-head">[Untitled]</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Legation</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Copenhagen</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1916-08-26T13:00:00+02:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">August 26, 1916, 1 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>331. Your 155, August 24th, 3 p.m. Owing to the widely current rumor here that
                    the United States is putting undue pressure on Denmark for the cession within a
                    time limit of the islands, I have been obliged to say authoritatively that there
                    is no such pressure. I referred to the words of the published treaty and said
                    that the only pressure was the expressed wish that the matter might be expedited
                    as soon as possible. Paris <hi rendition="#italic">New York Herald</hi> of
                    August 20th in an interview with a distinguished Dane echoes these rumors.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Egan</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
